<body>
  <a class="skip" href="#story">Skip to the story</a>
  <header class="masthead"><a href="/" class="author">Praneeth Paikray <span>/ field guides</span></a><div class="mast-actions"><a href="#sources">Notes & sources</a><button id="motion" type="button" aria-pressed="false">Pause motion</button></div></header>
  <main>
    <section class="cover" aria-labelledby="title">
      <div class="edition"><span>FIELD GUIDE 001</span><span>COMPUTATION, DRAWN BY HAND</span><span>01 NOV 2026</span></div>
      <h1 id="title">How a language<br/>model is <em>made.</em></h1>
      <p class="deck">Before the first answer, there is a very long apprenticeship.<br class="desktop"/> Follow the text, the numbers, and the work that changes them.</p>
      <div class="cover-key"><span><i class="dot terracotta"></i> Data moves</span><span><i class="dot blue"></i> Numbers transform</span><span><i class="dot green"></i> Weights learn</span></div>
      <div class="world" id="world"><div set:html={workshop()}></div><div class="map-caption"><span>FIG. 01 / THE LANGUAGE WORKSHOP</span><button type="button" id="map-open">Enlarge the drawing ↗</button><span>Choose a station. Follow what happens inside.</span></div></div>
      <div class="tour"><button type="button" id="tour-next">Follow the model →</button><p id="tour-caption" aria-live="polite">It starts with a collection of text, long before it can answer you.</p><span id="tour-count">01 / 07</span></div>
      <p class="scope">A common recipe for a text-only, decoder-only transformer. Real systems vary. The machinery is a metaphor; the operations and equations are real. All numerical labs are small teaching models, not measurements of a commercial LLM.</p>
    </section>
    <nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Story chapters">{chapters.map(([id,label],i)=><a href={'#'+id}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span>{label}</a>)}</nav>
    <div id="story">
      <section class="chapter" id="data" aria-labelledby="data-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">01 / GATHER & SELECT</p><h2 id="data-title">The internet is<br/><em>not a dataset.</em></h2><p>First, people choose what the model will encounter. Web pages, code, books, licensed material, and purpose-built examples can all be inputs. Access rights, privacy, language coverage, and intended use shape that choice.</p><p>A curation pipeline extracts text, removes unwanted material, detects duplicates, and sets the mixture. Repeated text can dominate training. Filters also change whose language and knowledge remain. <a class="cite" href="#source-1">[1]</a></p><p>Keep evaluation examples separate. A model that has already seen the test can appear more capable than it is.</p><div class="marginal">The first design decision is what to leave in.</div></div>
        <div class="lab" id="corpus-lab"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 02 / A VERY SMALL CORPUS</span><span class="lab-tag">OPERATE THE SORTING TABLE</span></div><div class="corpus-table" id="documents"></div><div class="lab-controls"><label><input type="checkbox" id="filter-quality" checked /> Remove spam</label><label><input type="checkbox" id="filter-duplicate" checked /> Deduplicate</label><label><input type="checkbox" id="filter-test" checked /> Reserve evaluation</label></div><p class="readout" id="corpus-status" aria-live="polite"></p><p class="fineprint">12 invented documents. Statuses are supplied labels, not an automated quality classifier. “Reserved” documents never join the training set while the split is enabled.</p></div>
        <details class="deep"><summary>Open the notebook / data quality is a choice</summary><div><p>Exact matching catches identical documents. Approximate matching can catch near-copies. Language detection, heuristic rules, and learned quality classifiers make different tradeoffs. FineWeb documents experiments with these choices rather than assuming that more aggressive filtering is always better. <a href="#source-1">[1]</a></p><p>Split by the unit that can leak: a source, a document family, a person, or time. Removing only exact test strings does not rule out near-duplicate contamination. Keep a record of data sources, exclusions, mixture weights, and versions so a later regression can be investigated.</p></div></details>
      </section>
      <section class="chapter" id="tokens" aria-labelledby="tokens-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">02 / BUILD A CODEBOOK</p><h2 id="tokens-title">Words become<br/><em>addresses.</em></h2><p>A tokenizer maps text to a sequence of integer IDs. A token may be a whole word, part of a word, punctuation, or bytes. The ID is an address in a vocabulary, not a measure of meaning.</p><p>Byte-pair encoding builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging frequent neighboring units. The finished tokenizer reuses learned rules on new text. Other algorithms, including Unigram and WordPiece, make different choices. <a class="cite" href="#source-2">[2]</a></p><p>Try learning a tiny codebook. Every merge below is computed from the current sequence.</p></div>
        <div class="lab token-lab"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 03 / LEARNING A TINY BPE VOCABULARY</span><span>TOY CHARACTER-LEVEL BPE</span></div><p class="sample-text">low lower low lowest</p><div id="token-strip" aria-label="Token sequence"></div><div class="lab-controls"><button type="button" id="merge">Merge the most frequent pair</button><button type="button" id="token-reset" class="text-button">Start again ↺</button></div><p class="readout" id="merge-status" aria-live="polite">Start with characters. Spaces are fixed boundaries in this toy.</p><div class="token-ledger"><span>UNITS IN THE SEQUENCE <b id="token-count">20</b></span><span>MERGE RULES LEARNED <b id="merge-count">0</b></span></div><p class="fineprint">This teaches merge learning, not a production tokenizer. Ties use first occurrence; merges do not cross spaces. Real pipelines may use bytes, normalization, pre-tokenization, and special tokens.</p></div>
        <details class="deep"><summary>Open the notebook / tokens become vectors</summary><div><p>The network looks up each token ID in a learned embedding matrix, E ∈ ℝ<sup>V×d</sup>. A sequence of T tokens becomes T vectors, each with d coordinates. All token embeddings usually learn during pretraining.</p><p>Position matters too. Different architectures add positional vectors or use mechanisms such as rotary position embeddings. The integers alone carry neither order nor semantic distance. Long documents are divided or packed into bounded training sequences, with attention masks and document boundaries handled according to the recipe. <a href="#source-3">[3]</a></p></div></details>
      </section>
      <section class="chapter engine-chapter" id="transformer" aria-labelledby="engine-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">03 / CONSTRUCT THE ENGINE</p><h2 id="engine-title">Each position<br/><em>reads its past.</em></h2><p>A decoder block combines two operations. Attention mixes information across allowed positions. A feed-forward network transforms the vector at each position. Residual paths preserve a route around these operations. Stacking blocks builds richer representations. <a class="cite" href="#source-4">[4]</a></p><p>The last representation projects to one score, or logit, per vocabulary item. Softmax turns those scores into a probability distribution for the next token.</p><p>The causal mask is essential. To predict token t + 1, position t can use only its prefix. It cannot read the answer.</p></div>
        <figure class="engine-art"><div set:html={engine()}></div><figcaption>FIG. 04 / A DECODER, OPENED UP<br/>Illustrative layers. For a common pre-norm block: h′ = h + Attention(Norm(h)); h″ = h′ + MLP(Norm(h′)). A final norm and vocabulary projection follow the stack. Position information is architecture-specific.</figcaption></figure>
        <div class="attention-lab lab full"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 05 / WATCH ONE ATTENTION HEAD</span><span>EXACT MATH ON INVENTED Q, K, V VECTORS</span></div><div class="attention-layout"><div><p class="hand-title">Which earlier positions can this one use?</p><div class="query-buttons" id="query-buttons"></div><p class="fineprint">Select a query position. A dot marks the query; crossed cells are future positions. The matrix shows all five query rows.</p><div class="formula">A = softmax(QKᵀ / √d<sub>k</sub> + mask)</div><div class="matrix-key"><i></i> More attention weight <span>× Future, blocked</span></div></div><div class="attention-matrix" id="attention-matrix" aria-label="Causal attention weights"></div><div class="attention-readout"><p class="eyebrow">SELECTED QUERY</p><p id="attention-query" class="hand-title"></p><div id="attention-bars"></div><p class="fineprint" id="attention-output"></p></div></div><p class="fineprint">The vectors are hand-chosen for a single head, not learned language representations. The row softmax and weighted sum are calculated. Attention weight is not a reliable explanation of a model’s reasoning.</p></div>
        <details class="deep"><summary>Open the notebook / what Q, K, and V actually do</summary><div><p>Learned projections create queries Q = XW<sub>Q</sub>, keys K = XW<sub>K</sub>, and values V = XW<sub>V</sub>. A query–key dot product produces a compatibility score. The scaled, masked softmax supplies coefficients for a weighted sum of value vectors. Multiple heads use different projections; their outputs are combined through another learned projection. <a href="#source-4">[4]</a></p><p>Modern decoder recipes differ in normalization, activation functions, positional encoding, attention variants, and whether the feed-forward component is dense or a mixture of experts. This guide uses a dense decoder as its running example. A layer is numerical computation, not a neuron with a human concept assigned to it. <a href="#source-3">[3]</a></p></div></details>
      </section>
      <section class="chapter" id="learning" aria-labelledby="learning-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">04 / CHANGE THE WEIGHTS</p><h2 id="learning-title">Predict.<br/>Compare.<br/><em>Adjust.</em></h2><p>Pretraining begins with initialized parameters. Given a prefix, the model predicts the next token. The actual continuation supplies the target. No person has to label every word.</p><p>Cross-entropy penalizes low probability on that target. Backpropagation computes how each trainable parameter affected the loss. An optimizer uses those gradients to update the parameters. Repeat across batches. <a class="cite" href="#source-3">[3]</a></p><p>Run an update on this three-choice prediction. Watch the target probability rise as the loss falls.</p></div>
        <div class="lab learning-lab"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 06 / A REAL GRADIENT STEP, IN MINIATURE</span><span>THREE TRAINABLE LOGITS</span></div><p class="hand-title">“The sky is <span class="blank">blue</span>”</p><p class="target-note">The training text says “blue”. That is the target for this example.</p><div id="learn-bars"></div><div class="loss-equation">loss = −ln p(<span>blue</span>) = <strong id="loss">1.10</strong></div><div class="lab-controls"><button type="button" id="learn-step">Take one gradient step →</button><button type="button" id="learn-reset" class="text-button">Reset ↺</button><label for="learn-rate">Step size <input id="learn-rate" type="range" min="0.05" max="1.5" step="0.05" value="0.5" /><output id="rate-value">0.50</output></label></div><svg id="loss-plot" viewBox="0 0 520 130" role="img" aria-label="Cross-entropy loss across toy optimization steps"></svg><p class="readout" id="learn-status" aria-live="polite">Step 0. All three choices are equally likely.</p><p class="fineprint">We optimize the logits directly with gradient descent: z′ = z − η(p − y). A transformer instead backpropagates through its layers and typically uses an optimizer such as AdamW. This curve is generated by this toy, not a training benchmark.</p></div>
        <details class="deep"><summary>Open the notebook / a batch predicts many next tokens</summary><div><p>In teacher-forced training, the full known sequence enters the network. The causal mask lets the network calculate predictions at many positions in parallel. Targets are the input sequence shifted by one position. The loss averages valid target positions, then often averages or accumulates across examples.</p><p>Gradients, activations, model weights, and optimizer state consume memory. Mixed precision, gradient accumulation, activation checkpointing, learning-rate schedules, and gradient clipping help make large runs feasible. Save optimizer and scheduler state with checkpoints if the run must resume faithfully. Training loss can fall while held-out performance worsens. <a href="#source-3">[3]</a></p></div></details>
      </section>
      <section class="chapter scale-chapter" id="scale" aria-labelledby="scale-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">05 / REPEAT, AT SCALE</p><h2 id="scale-title">One update.<br/><em>Many machines.</em></h2><p>Large training runs distribute work. Data parallel workers process different batches and combine gradients. Tensor parallelism splits operations within layers. Pipeline parallelism splits layers. Sharding can divide weights, gradients, and optimizer state. Communication and failures become part of the training problem. <a class="cite" href="#source-3">[3]</a></p><p>Model size and training tokens both matter. A larger parameter count alone does not tell you how well a model has been trained. Compute-optimal training and economical serving can favor different choices. <a class="cite" href="#source-5">[5]</a></p></div>
        <div class="cluster-panel"><div set:html={cluster()}></div><div class="compute-controls"><label for="parameters">Parameters <output id="parameters-value">7B</output><input id="parameters" type="range" min="1" max="70" value="7" /></label><label for="training-tokens">Training tokens <output id="training-tokens-value">1T</output><input id="training-tokens" type="range" min="1" max="150" value="10" /></label><div class="compute-result"><span>APPROXIMATE DENSE TRAINING COMPUTE</span><strong id="compute-value">42 ZFLOP</strong><span>C ≈ 6ND</span></div></div><p class="fineprint">An order-of-magnitude approximation for dense transformer training, with N parameters and D tokens. One ZFLOP is 10²¹ floating-point operations. Attention costs, embedding details, recomputation, utilization, and networking alter the actual cost. This is not a runtime or price estimate. <a href="#source-5">[5]</a></p></div>
      </section>
      <div class="interlude"><span>THE WEIGHTS NOW CONTAIN A LANGUAGE MODEL.</span><p>Continuing text and<br/><em>answering a person</em><br/>are different training goals.</p><span>ENTER POST-TRAINING ↓</span></div>
      <section class="chapter" id="assistant" aria-labelledby="assistant-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">06 / LEARN FROM DEMONSTRATIONS</p><h2 id="assistant-title">Show it<br/><em>a good answer.</em></h2><p>Supervised fine-tuning, or SFT, trains on examples of desired behavior. A chat template encodes roles and turn boundaries. The model still predicts tokens, but the training examples now show how an assistant should respond. <a class="cite" href="#source-6">[6]</a></p><p>A common chat SFT recipe computes loss on assistant responses and masks the prompt. Select the two objectives to see which target tokens count. The prompt remains visible to attention even when its loss is masked.</p><div class="marginal">A loss mask and an attention mask do different jobs.</div></div>
        <div class="lab sft-lab"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 07 / SAME PREDICTION TASK, DIFFERENT TARGETS</span><span>SCHEMATIC TOKEN GROUPS</span></div><div class="segmented" aria-label="Training objective"><button type="button" id="mode-pretrain" aria-pressed="false">Pretraining</button><button type="button" id="mode-sft" aria-pressed="true">Assistant-only SFT</button></div><div class="chat-example"><div><span class="role">USER</span><p><span data-chat-token="prompt">Explain</span> <span data-chat-token="prompt">rain</span> <span data-chat-token="prompt">simply.</span></p></div><div><span class="role">ASSISTANT</span><p><span data-chat-token="answer">Water</span> <span data-chat-token="answer">drops</span> <span data-chat-token="answer">fall</span> <span data-chat-token="answer">from</span> <span data-chat-token="answer">clouds.</span></p></div></div><p class="readout" id="sft-status" aria-live="polite">Only the five assistant token groups contribute to loss. All prompt groups supply context.</p><p class="fineprint">Tint means “this group’s next-token target contributes to loss”. Groups simplify token boundaries; role markers and end-of-turn tokens are omitted. SFT recipes can also train on other selected turns or full sequences.</p></div>
        <details class="deep"><summary>Open the notebook / examples can come from people or models</summary><div><p>Demonstrations may be written by people, generated by another model, filtered, and revised. Their quality and coverage set the behavior the model is asked to imitate. SFT can update all parameters or use parameter-efficient methods. Domain-adaptive continued pretraining has a different objective from teaching assistant behavior.</p><p>A convincing response is not proof that the underlying statement is true. Training the tone and format of an answer does not supply missing evidence. Grounded generation, retrieval, tools, and separate verification can address different parts of that problem. <a href="#source-6">[6]</a></p></div></details>
      </section>
      <section class="chapter" id="preferences" aria-labelledby="preferences-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">07 / LEARN FROM COMPARISONS & OUTCOMES</p><h2 id="preferences-title">An example says how.<br/><em>Feedback says which.</em></h2><p>A preference pair contains a prompt, a preferred response, and a rejected response. DPO trains the policy directly on these comparisons, relative to a reference policy. It does not require a separately trained reward model. <a class="cite" href="#source-7">[7]</a></p><p>In a classic RLHF route, preferences first train a reward model. An RL optimizer then adjusts the policy using its scores, usually with a constraint on drift. <a class="cite" href="#source-6">[6]</a></p><p>Some tasks instead offer checkable outcomes, such as a passed test or a verified answer. Reinforcement learning can use those rewards, but the checking rule can itself be incomplete. <a class="cite" href="#source-10">[10]</a></p></div>
        <div class="lab preference-lab"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 08 / A PAIRWISE PREFERENCE UPDATE</span><span>ONE-DIMENSIONAL DPO TOY</span></div><p class="hand-title">“Can you tell me tomorrow’s exact stock price?”</p><div class="response-pair"><div class="chosen"><span>CHOSEN</span><p>“I can’t know the exact future price. We can examine scenarios and their assumptions.”</p></div><div class="rejected"><span>REJECTED</span><p>“Yes. It will be exactly ₹1,250.”</p></div></div><div class="preference-scale"><span>Rejected</span><div><i id="preference-marker"></i></div><span>Chosen</span></div><div class="lab-controls"><button type="button" id="dpo-step">Apply a preference update →</button><button type="button" id="dpo-reset" class="text-button">Reset ↺</button></div><p class="readout" id="dpo-status" aria-live="polite"></p><p class="fineprint">We optimize only the pair’s log-probability margin m = log π(chosen) − log π(rejected), with reference margin 0, β = 0.5, and step size 1. The marker is σ(m), a relative score for this pair, not an LLM’s response probability or factual accuracy.</p></div>
        <details class="deep"><summary>Open the notebook / three routes, not one mandatory pipeline</summary><div class="route-notes"><p><strong>DPO.</strong> A pairwise loss increases the chosen response’s likelihood relative to the rejected response and the reference. Here, L = −log σ(β(m − m<sub>ref</sub>)). Real training differentiates sequence log probabilities through the policy. <a href="#source-7">[7]</a></p><p><strong>Reward-model RLHF.</strong> Train a reward predictor on comparisons, sample responses, score them, and optimize the policy. Reward hacking is possible when high reward does not match the intended behavior. <a href="#source-6">[6]</a></p><p><strong>Verifiable-reward RL.</strong> A checker provides a reward for an outcome, for example mathematical answer correctness. This can improve reasoning behavior without making every output correct. DeepSeek-R1 reports such an approach. Model families may combine these routes, repeat stages, or use other methods. <a href="#source-10">[10]</a></p></div></details>
      </section>
      <section class="chapter" id="evaluation" aria-labelledby="evaluation-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">08 / EARN THE NEXT CHECKPOINT</p><h2 id="evaluation-title">A lower loss<br/><em>isn’t a release.</em></h2><p>A checkpoint is a saved model state, not proof that it is ready for people. Teams evaluate held-out capability, instruction following, robustness, safety, and regressions. Automated graders, executable tests, and human reviews each have limits. <a class="cite" href="#source-3">[3]</a></p><p>This miniature release gate has explicit rules. Break one and the model returns for more work. Passing these few checks is only permission to advance in this teaching model.</p><p>The lifecycle can loop. Evaluation can reveal a data problem, an optimization problem, or a post-training problem.</p></div>
        <div class="lab evaluation-lab"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 09 / A GATE WITH RULES</span><span>ILLUSTRATIVE RELEASE POLICY</span></div><div class="eval-controls"><label><input type="checkbox" id="eval-leak" checked /> Evaluation set is isolated</label><label><input type="checkbox" id="eval-capability" checked /> Capability target is met</label><label><input type="checkbox" id="eval-safety" checked /> Required safety checks pass</label><label><input type="checkbox" id="eval-regression" checked /> Regression budget is met</label></div><div id="gate-result" class="gate-result" role="status"></div><div class="state-ribbon"><span>TRAIN</span><b>→</b><span>CHECK</span><b>→</b><span id="gate-state">SERVE</span></div><p class="fineprint">These are four invented Boolean requirements, not empirical results or a complete safety assessment. The page’s executable lifecycle model checks that release cannot precede evaluation and that inference does not update weights. <a href="/llm/lifecycle.json">Inspect the checked graph ↗</a></p></div>
      </section>
      <section class="chapter inference-chapter" id="inference" aria-labelledby="inference-title">
        <div class="chapter-copy"><p class="eyebrow">09 / PUT IT TO WORK</p><h2 id="inference-title">The learning stops.<br/><em>The context grows.</em></h2><p>In ordinary inference, model weights stay fixed. Prefill processes the prompt. Decode predicts a distribution, selects a token, appends it, and repeats until an end condition.</p><p>A KV cache reuses earlier attention keys and values. It saves recomputation; it is not a weight update or a guarantee of long-term memory. <a class="cite" href="#source-8">[8]</a></p><p>Temperature changes the sampling distribution. Top-p retains a smallest high-probability prefix whose cumulative mass reaches the threshold, then renormalizes. These controls change selection, not what the model has learned. <a class="cite" href="#source-9">[9]</a></p><div class="serving-art" set:html={serving()}></div></div>
        <div class="lab inference-lab"><div class="figline"><span>FIG. 10 / THE LAST FEW MILLISECONDS</span><span>SCRIPTED LOGITS, REAL SAMPLING</span></div><div class="terminal"><span>PROMPT + GENERATED CONTEXT</span><p id="generated-text">The sky is</p><div class="cursor-line"><i></i></div></div><div class="sampling-controls"><label for="temperature">Temperature <output id="temperature-value">0.80</output><input id="temperature" type="range" min="0" max="2" step="0.05" value="0.8" /></label><label for="top-p">Top-p <output id="top-p-value">0.90</output><input id="top-p" type="range" min="0.1" max="1" step="0.05" value="0.9" /></label></div><div id="inference-bars"></div><div class="lab-controls"><button type="button" id="decode">Prefill the prompt →</button><button type="button" id="decode-reset" class="text-button">New request ↺</button></div><div class="cache-ledger"><span>STATE <b id="decode-state">READY</b></span><span>CACHED POSITIONS <b id="cache-size">0</b></span><span>WEIGHT UPDATES <b>0</b></span></div><p class="readout" id="decode-status" aria-live="polite">First, prefill the three-token toy prompt. Then sample one token at a time.</p><p class="fineprint">A finite scripted vocabulary supplies the logits; no LLM runs here. Softmax, top-p filtering, and random sampling are calculated in your browser. Spaces and words are simplified as tokens. A newly sampled token enters the KV cache only when processed by the next forward pass.</p></div>
        <details class="deep"><summary>Open the notebook / the model and the system around it</summary><div><p>A deployable model includes weights, an architecture configuration, and the matching tokenizer and chat template. Serving adds request scheduling, batching, memory management, and monitoring. Quantization changes numerical representation and may change quality.</p><p>Retrieval supplies documents in the context. Tools execute operations outside the language model. Conversation state supplies prior turns. Those system behaviors are separate from training the weights. Production feedback can later enter a curated training or evaluation dataset, but an ordinary chat request does not automatically perform that training.</p></div></details>
      </section>
    </div>
    <section class="checked-model"><p class="eyebrow">ONE PROCESS, WITH LOOPS AND BRANCHES</p><h2>The model’s <em>other model.</em></h2><p>A small TLA+ specification describes this development lifecycle. Poteto’s statechart skill ran TLC over <strong>36 reachable states and 62 steps</strong>. All four stated invariants held; no deadlocks were found. The drawing merges these into 11 visible states and 15 arrows, and hides 18 steps that stay within the same visible state.</p><details><summary>Unfold the checked lifecycle chart</summary><img src="/llm/lifecycle.svg" alt="Checked lifecycle statechart. Collected text becomes curated text, then token sequences and a base model. Optional assistant and preference training lead to evaluation. Evaluation can return to collection or advance to a released checkpoint, followed by prefill, generation, and request completion." loading="lazy" /><p><a href="/llm/lifecycle.mmd">Mermaid text</a> · <a href="/llm/Lifecycle.tla">TLA+ model</a> · <a href="/llm/Lifecycle.cfg">Configuration</a> · <a href="/llm/lifecycle-report.md">TLC report</a></p><p>This abstract model assumes evaluation has completed before release. It does not model the truth of an evaluation result, gradient quality, distributed failures, or the behavior of a real language model.</p><p>To reproduce with the pstack statechart skill: <code>statechart Lifecycle.tla --view lifecycle-view.mjs --out new-check</code>. Use a new output directory.</p></details></section>
    <section class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT THE WORKSHOP LEAVES BEHIND</p><h2>A codebook.<br/>A set of learned weights.<br/><em>A reason to keep checking.</em></h2><p>The answer on your screen is the last step of the story.<br/>Every earlier choice helps determine which answer becomes likely.</p><a href="#world">Return to the workshop ↑</a></section>
    <section id="sources" class="sources"><div class="sources-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE NOTEBOOK / SOURCES & BOUNDARIES</p><h2>Look under<br/><em>the drawings.</em></h2><p>Written and illustrated by Praneeth Paikray. Published 1 November 2026. This is a teaching model of a common development process, not a description of every LLM or a report of a new experiment.</p><p>Visual direction takes inspiration from Kevin T. Ngo’s <a href="https://kevintngo.github.io/statechart-art/one-becomes-two/">One becomes two</a>, April Zhu’s <a href="https://april-zhu.com/fieldnotes">Field Notes</a> and <a href="https://april-zhu.com/colorfigures">Colorfigures</a>. The original workshop drawings are code-generated SVG. Kevin Sans is reused under the <a href="/llm/STATECHART-ART-LICENSE.txt">statechart-art MIT license</a>.</p><p>Built using Poteto’s <a href="https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/pstack">pstack</a> guidance on experience, domain models, verification, and writing. The small lifecycle graph is exhaustively checked by the included JavaScript checker. It does not prove that a trained model is correct or safe.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Praneeth16/Praneeth16.github.io/tree/main/src/pages/blog/how-llms-are-built">Page source ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Praneeth16/Praneeth16.github.io/blob/main/scripts/check-llm-story.mjs">Math & lifecycle checks ↗</a></p></div><ol>
      <li id="source-1"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.17557">Penedo et al. · The FineWeb Datasets</a><p>Data extraction, filtering, deduplication, and ablations.</p></li>
      <li id="source-2"><a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/tokenizer_summary">Hugging Face · Tokenization algorithms</a><p>BPE, WordPiece, Unigram, and the difference between words and tokens.</p></li>
      <li id="source-3"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21783">Llama team · The Llama 3 Herd of Models</a><p>A concrete dense-decoder recipe, training infrastructure, post-training, and evaluations.</p></li>
      <li id="source-4"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762">Vaswani et al. · Attention Is All You Need</a><p>Scaled dot-product attention, multiple heads, and causal masking. The original paper uses an encoder–decoder; this story follows a decoder-only variant.</p></li>
      <li id="source-5"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556">Hoffmann et al. · Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models</a><p>Balancing parameters and tokens under a training compute budget.</p></li>
      <li id="source-6"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155">Ouyang et al. · Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback</a><p>Demonstrations, reward modeling, and policy optimization.</p></li>
      <li id="source-7"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290">Rafailov et al. · Direct Preference Optimization</a><p>The reference-relative pairwise objective used in the preference lab.</p></li>
      <li id="source-8"><a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/cache_explanation">Hugging Face · How caching works</a><p>Attention key/value reuse during autoregressive inference.</p></li>
      <li id="source-9"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09751">Holtzman et al. · The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration</a><p>Nucleus sampling and the role of decoding choices.</p></li>
      <li id="source-10"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948">DeepSeek-AI · DeepSeek-R1</a><p>Reasoning-oriented reinforcement learning and verifiable rewards.</p></li>
    </ol></section>
